// Capital Z - letterform study, 25 variants. Nothing here ships on its own; the winner becomes the
// character inside sort-ascending / sort-descending and their alphabetical variants.
//
// THE SLOT, from the study brief: two characters stack inside the mark with 1.5 of air between them,
// so one character gets 8.25 of ink at 24, 6.25 at 20 and 5.25 at 16. Ink height = centreline height
// + the stroke, so the bars sit 6.75 / 4.75 / 4.0 apart, centreline to centreline, in every candidate
// on this board. 16 was drawn first. Bars are on y 8.75 / 15.5 at 24, 7.75 / 12.5 at 20 and
// 5.625 / 9.625 at 16 - the 16 pair is on the .625 eighth so each bar renders as one solid pixel row
// plus a quarter, instead of two grey rows (centreline 6.0 would have split 62/62).
//
// AXES, AND WHAT EACH COST. 7 on the diagonal's angle (A-G), which is the character; 4 on bar lengths
// (H-K); 4 on where the diagonal meets the bars (L-O); 2 on the corner (P, Q); 4 on the barred Z
// (R-U); 2 on giving each master its own angle (V, W); 2 singles (X, Y).
//
// THE ONE NUMBER THAT DECIDES THIS BOARD. A Z's counter is the wedge between a bar and the diagonal.
// Its air is the perpendicular distance from the bar's free end to the diagonal, less one stroke:
//     air = H x L / sqrt(L^2 + H^2) - stroke,   H = centreline height, L = the diagonal's run.
// Setting that to 1.5 gives the steepest Z each master can hold:
//     24 -> 63.6 degrees     20 -> 50.8 degrees     16 -> 46.6 degrees
// 16 is the wall. Every candidate's measured air is in its note; anything under 1.5 is named there.
//
// MEASURED FROM THE REFERENCES THIS SESSION (fontTools, the system font files - input, not rules):
// a real capital Z runs its diagonal at 51.4 (Helvetica), 51.2 (Arial), 51.0 (Verdana), 54.4 (SF),
// 54.7 (Tahoma), 60.2 (Futura) degrees, with ink 0.79 / 0.79 / 0.78 / 0.74 / 0.70 / 0.74 as wide as
// tall. EVERY ONE OF THEM IS STEEPER THAN OUR 16 MASTER CAN HOLD. The reason is weight, not taste:
// a text face carries a stroke about 0.12 of its cap height, we carry 0.24 at 16 (1.25 into 5.25),
// so the same skeleton leaves half the air. Our Z has to be 20-25% wider than a text Z at 16, and
// that is a fact about the slot, not a preference.
//
// TWO THINGS THE BOARD CLOSES RATHER THAN OFFERS.
//   * THE CORNER IS NOT A CHOICE. The root sets stroke-linejoin round, so every corner already is
//     C10's arc at half the weight; a sharp mitre cannot be drawn without changing the root. The only
//     move left is a LARGER drawn arc, and at 16 the smallest legal one (0.625) already spends 1.51
//     of the 4.0 bar (P), while 0.875 spends 2.11 and the letter reads as a 2 (Q).
//   * THE BARRED Z IS NOT AVAILABLE BELOW 24. A crossbar needs 1.5 of air plus a stroke on each side:
//     5.5 of centreline at 16 and 6.0 at 20, against the 4.0 and 4.75 the slot gives. Only 24, with
//     6.75, has room. R, S and T carry the measured failure; U is the rule working - barred at 24,
//     plain below - which is the honest reading that this set cannot carry a crossed Z at all.
//
// THE FILLED FILE. The study brief asked for `filled: 'outline'` - the character keeps the OUTLINE
// stroke in both styles, the way list-ordered-filled keeps its numerals at 1.25 while its bars fill.
// That keyword is retired in draft-svg.mjs, and a filled file with no filled area is held to
// FILLED_WEIGHT (2.5 / 2 / 2), which is the thickened letter this study exists to avoid. So each
// filled file is the SAME LETTER traced: the stroked outline converted to one even-odd fill per
// path, round caps and joins included. It renders pixel for pixel as the outline does - checked by
// rasterising both at 16, 20 and 24 (largest difference 0.03 of one pixel's coverage, which is the
// checker's own arc sampling) - and draft-check reports the two ink boxes identical on all 25.
//
// WHAT LINT SEES AND DOES NOT. The three warnings on the board are all on the barred group, all on
// the filled file, and all the crossbar: 0.75 at 20 and 16 for R and S, and 0.69 / 0.04 / 0.22 for
// T. They are the study's evidence, not defects to fix. Lint CANNOT see the thing this board is
// about: a Z's counter is an open wedge, so section 5b's turn gate skips it and section 5f does not
// read it as two walls facing square-on. Every counter number here was measured by hand and by the
// rasteriser. A clean draft-check proves nothing about a letterform.
//
// THE OUTER SILHOUETTE IS ONE UNBROKEN LINE, CHECKED NOT ASSUMED. Twenty of the twenty-five draw
// the Z as a SINGLE path, so both corners are stroke-linejoin round joins and there is no connector
// to show. The five that cannot (L, M, N, O, X - an inset or an overshooting diagonal, or a lower
// bar that overhangs) were measured junction by junction: each round cap was sampled over +-80
// degrees (its two 90-degree extremes are its own stroke's side edges, where the arc is tangent)
// against the other stroke's ink, at all three masters.
//   * ONE REAL LUMP WAS FOUND, on O. A bar that simply stopped near the diagonal left its round cap
//     0.013 at 24, 0.018 at 20 and 0.021 at 16 PAST the diagonal's edge - the same defect as the A
//     board's crossbar. A diagonal's edge sits (stroke/2)x|dy|/length from its centreline in x, NOT
//     stroke/2, so a horizontal bar sized as though it were stroke/2 always overshoots.
//   * THE OTHER TEN JUNCTIONS MEASURED 0.0000 - not proud, but decided by a coincidence of two
//     equal stroke widths, which is exactly the fragile case.
//   BOTH ARE FIXED THE SAME WAY, AND IT IS NOT A TUCK. A cap that has to be buried is a cap that
//   should not be there: in all five the diagonal now RUNS OUT along the bar - onto the bar's own
//   end in L, M, N and X, onto the diagonal's centreline where the two cross in O - so every
//   junction is a round JOIN, and the only free caps left are the ones that are supposed to show:
//   the bars' outer ends, and O's two overshoot nubs. Re-measured: no cap stands proud anywhere on
//   the board, at any master. A first attempt DID tuck each buried cap one grid step in; it worked
//   optically but put five candidates 0.13-0.25 apart, which lint section 5 reads as a gap between
//   two paths and warns on. Sharing the cap instead keeps the paths touching and the board quiet.
//   Nothing else moved: not one angle, counter measurement or ink box on this board changed.
//
// Every number in this file was measured this session: the drawings by a generator and an exact
// 16x16 rasteriser in the session scratchpad, the reference angles from the font outlines, the ink
// boxes by tools/draft-check.mjs. The OUTLINE files are on their master's grid except P and Q (arc
// tangent points) and T (a bar square to a 45-degree spine); the FILLED files carry off-grid values
// throughout, because a traced round cap on a 45-degree end does not land on a quarter or an
// eighth. All are documented exceptions under lint section 3.

